-- title:
--   Eisenstein: algebraic power series have geometrically bounded coefficients
-- statement:
--   Let $\varphi \in \mathbb{C}[[X]]$ be a formal power series in one variable over $\mathbb{C}$, and assume that $\varphi$ is algebraic over the polynomial ring $\mathbb{C}[X]$ in the sense of `IsAlgebraic`, i.e. there is a nonzero polynomial $P \in (\mathbb{C}[X])[Y]$ in one variable with coefficients in $\mathbb{C}[X]$, the latter acting on $\mathbb{C}[[X]]$ through the canonical algebra structure, such that $P(\varphi) = 0$ in $\mathbb{C}[[X]]$. The conclusion asserts the existence of two real numbers $C$ and $R$ such that for every natural number $n$ the $n$-th coefficient of $\varphi$ satisfies $\|\mathrm{coeff}_n(\varphi)\| \le C\,R^{n}$, the norm being the absolute value on $\mathbb{C}$. No positivity or normalisation is imposed on $C$ and $R$, and none is needed: the inequality for all $n$ forces $C\,R^{n} \ge 0$, so it yields the geometric majorant $\|\mathrm{coeff}_n(\varphi)\| \le |C|\,|R|^{n}$. Thus the coefficients of $\varphi$ grow at most geometrically; the statement is the coefficient bound itself rather than the derived assertion that $\varphi$ has positive radius of convergence and defines a holomorphic germ at $0$.
--
--   This is Eisenstein's theorem on the coefficients of algebraic power series, in its quantitative form. It supplies the analytic input for the study of places of a complex algebraic curve: it is used in [`AlgebraicCurve.Place.exists_localParam_meromorphicOrderAt_evalAt_eq_ord_complex`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.Place.exists_localParam_meromorphicOrderAt_evalAt_eq_ord_complex), where series expansions of functions at a place, being algebraic over the field generated by a local parameter, must be shown to converge so that the parameter becomes a genuine local coordinate.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_PowerSeries_exists_norm_coeff_le_mul_pow_of_isAlgebraic_complex.lean

import Mathlib

